WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.781
In 1879, children kept getting lost in the crowds going up to a shrine in Nagasaki.

00:00:04.781 --> 00:00:08.877
So the local police officers pooled their own money and put up a stone.

00:00:08.877 --> 00:00:12.388
If you'd lost your child, you wrote their name on one side.

00:00:12.388 --> 00:00:16.484
If you'd found one, you wrote your own address on the other, and waited.

00:00:17.034 --> 00:00:21.991
I only know this because a stranger wrote it in a Google review, in Japanese, eight years ago.

00:00:21.991 --> 00:00:23.643
Look at the top of it.

00:00:23.643 --> 00:00:24.194
Three stars.

00:00:24.744 --> 00:00:26.537
I went to Nagasaki in October.

00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:29.527
Every map I opened gave me stars and closing times.

00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:31.619
I like to walk, so I walked.

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:36.190
I walked into an old coffee shop and got talking with a couple from Sydney.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.139
The owner told us about the art all over the walls.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.748
Her husband made all of it.

00:00:41.298 --> 00:00:46.110
I found a tiny sara udon shop you'd walk straight past, run by an old man.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:51.223
One reviewer wasn't even sure it was open, because its noren hangs half over a vending machine.

00:00:51.772 --> 00:00:57.892
And a matcha cafe where the owner hangs up adoption posters for rescue cats, because of their love for cats.

00:00:58.443 --> 00:01:03.656
After that, I got the chance to visit Shimabara, an old castle town at the foot of a volcano.

00:01:04.206 --> 00:01:07.994
I stayed at a homestay called Bravo World, and the owner was so warm.

00:01:07.994 --> 00:01:09.617
I asked him about the name.

00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:14.217
He told me it comes from a World Cup win he loved, and bravo was the cheer.

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:18.112
We had a welcome party there, and I met some locals.

00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:22.066
One of them used to be a police officer, then became a teacher.

00:01:22.066 --> 00:01:28.148
They teach kendo, the Japanese sword art, and they were in a promo video for the local somen eating contest.

00:01:28.148 --> 00:01:30.886
Somen are the thin noodles Shimabara is famous for.

00:01:31.436 --> 00:01:37.333
At the same party, I learned that Shimabara has its own dialect, different from the Japanese I'd usually hear.

00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:40.126
I only found that out by talking to people.

00:01:40.676 --> 00:01:44.594
Then I made candles at a wax workshop, run by the cutest couple.

00:01:44.594 --> 00:01:46.102
They're the third generation there.

00:01:46.102 --> 00:01:49.417
He's seventh dan in kendo too, if I heard that right.

00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:54.541
And one day, they put up a sign saying they were closed, because his wife was angry.

00:01:55.091 --> 00:01:57.827
You won't get any of that from a star rating.

00:01:57.827 --> 00:01:59.196
That's why I built Noren.

00:01:59.196 --> 00:02:01.658
A noren is the curtain in a shop's doorway.

00:02:01.658 --> 00:02:05.763
On this map, a yellow one means somebody wrote down a story about that place.

00:02:06.313 --> 00:02:09.345
Most places have more than one of these, scattered all over.

00:02:09.345 --> 00:02:11.826
Nobody's going to read all of that before lunch.

00:02:11.826 --> 00:02:15.133
So AI reads them and writes what a friend would tell you.

00:02:15.684 --> 00:02:16.539
Here's the page.

00:02:16.539 --> 00:02:18.533
AI wrote it, and it says so.

00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:21.667
Every sentence links back to the real person it came from.

00:02:22.916 --> 00:02:24.692
Reviews only catch some of it.

00:02:24.692 --> 00:02:26.764
The rest is still in people's heads.

00:02:26.764 --> 00:02:28.836
So share it where you already post.

00:02:28.836 --> 00:02:30.317
Nobody needs one more app.

00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:36.533
Your friends are on Instagram, your family's on Facebook, and maybe you write a blog or build up your Google reviews.

00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:37.421
Paste the link.

00:02:37.421 --> 00:02:40.381
Your post stays yours, and Noren sends readers to you.

00:02:42.532 --> 00:02:44.967
Or write it straight onto Noren, with your photos.

00:02:47.216 --> 00:02:48.862
Here's mine, on that coffee shop.

00:02:49.413 --> 00:02:52.187
And nobody has checked most of this map yet.

00:02:52.187 --> 00:02:54.344
Waze asks drivers, is that still there?

00:02:54.344 --> 00:02:56.194
Noren asks you about a shop.

00:02:56.194 --> 00:03:01.434
One tap, and that shop's stamp goes in your stamp book, like the ones at Japanese temples.

00:03:01.434 --> 00:03:04.208
The whole neighbourhood gets one place closer to done.

00:03:04.757 --> 00:03:06.350
Don't stop at the stamp, though.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.475
Tell us what happened while you were there.

00:03:08.475 --> 00:03:10.865
It might be nothing like what happened to me.

00:03:10.865 --> 00:03:13.255
Moment by moment, we write a place's story together.

00:03:13.805 --> 00:03:15.596
Remember the art on the walls?

00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:17.984
I heard about it from the owner herself.

00:03:17.984 --> 00:03:23.358
Here's the same story, from a stranger's review: the art decorating the cafe was made by the master.

00:03:23.908 --> 00:03:25.264
The stories are out there.

00:03:25.264 --> 00:03:26.621
Somebody already wrote them down.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:32.888
Next time you walk through a noren, that somebody could be you.

